// Expected: a0 at one session and at two.
//
// Symmetric challenge-response where Bob binds his MAC to both nonces. With
// one session per role the only term that passes Alice's ASSERT is Bob's
// honest m1: forging MAC(k, CONCAT(na, x)) needs k, and the one oracle
// available — Bob himself — returns the honest m1 for the honest na, which
// is a replay and not an authentication attack.
//
// With --sessions 2 the attacker can route Alice's na to session 2's Bob and
// bring his answer back, so Alice accepts MAC(k, CONCAT(na, nb#2)). That is
// still not an attack. Bob#2 is another run of the same Bob under the same
// peer binding; it computed the MAC itself from the challenge Alice actually
// sent; the attacker never learns k, forges nothing, and no Bob send is
// accepted twice. Alice's run has exactly one matching Bob run, which is what
// injective agreement asks for — the expansion's static pairing of session 1
// with session 1 is an analyzer artifact, not a protocol requirement.
//
// session_replay_breaks_injectivity.vp is the counterweight: there the value
// delivered is a sibling run's own honest output, which that run's own partner
// also accepts, so one send has two acceptances and the query fails.
// session_peer_run_matches.vp is the same shape as this one with an
// authenticated first flight, so the matching run has a check to satisfy
// before it will answer at all.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private k
	generates na
]

Alice -> Bob: na

principal Bob[
	knows private k
	generates nb
	m1 = MAC(k, CONCAT(na, nb))
]

Bob -> Alice: m1, nb

principal Alice[
	_ = ASSERT(m1, MAC(k, CONCAT(na, nb)))?
]

queries[
	authentication? Bob -> Alice: m1
]
